From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] renderer: the AMD/RADV correctness fixes are on by default

Three corrections this tree already carries behind opt-in switches are wrong to leave off on the
device it runs on. Fritchii's fork carries all three as ordinary renderer behaviour, and the PS5 is
RADV on RDNA2, so each switch's own stated precondition is met.

1. EDEN_NX2_AMD_SHADER_FIXES, now on. Two SPIR-V lowering corrections:
   - A 64/128-bit storage access whose offset is not known to be a multiple of the access size is
     split into 32-bit words instead of taking a vector view. The binding base is aligned only to
     the device's storage-buffer alignment - 4 bytes on RADV - so the vector view reads the vector
     before it. The file's own words are "it reads the vector before (Ocarina's lighting)".
   - FP min/max/clamp/saturate emit the NaN-defining OpNMin/OpNMax, because Maxwell's FMNMX and .SAT
     return the non-NaN operand while Vulkan's FMin/FMax/FClamp leave NaN undefined and AMD's
     compiler passes it through. A NaN arriving in a fog or light chain then spreads to the target.

2. EDEN_NX2_BLEND_ALWAYS, now on. Native Switch 2 paths write blend registers without raising
   Dirty::Blending, which leaves a stale blend state in the pipeline key on a device without dynamic
   blend. The code already sits inside `if (!extended_dynamic_state_3_blend)`, and this device reports
   dynamic state level 2, so it is always in that branch. The fix is Fritchii's, and its comment
   names Ocarina's forest fog rendering black there.

3. EDEN_NX2_PLAIN_SAMPLER, now on. A compare-enabled TSC read by a plain, non-Dref sample: Maxwell
   ignores the comparison, Vulkan leaves it undefined, and AMD returns the 0/1 compare result, which
   is Ocarina's forest-fog composite reading alpha 0. The sampler variant now drops the comparison.

Each keeps its variable as a live escape: setting it to 0 turns that fix off, so a regression can be
bisected on the console without a new build.

What this can break. (1) re-emits SPIR-V for every affected program, and the split into 32-bit words
costs instructions on paths that used a vector view - the price Fritchii pays too. It also applies to
every title, not only Ocarina, which is intended: the alignment and NaN semantics are wrong for all
of them on this device. (2) and (3) change pipeline and sampler variant keys, so they add variants
rather than remove them: (3) can add a sampler variant per compare-enabled image, and (2) refreshes
blend state on every pipeline update in that branch instead of only when the dirty flag is set.

The disk cache is versioned for exactly this, so CACHE_VERSION goes from 23 to 24. A cache written by
an earlier build holds the old SPIR-V for these programs and would otherwise be loaded as it is,
hiding the whole change. The first boot after this build will re-translate and re-cache what it needs.

Measured or a guess: the preconditions are measured - RADV aligns storage bindings to 4 bytes, this
device reports dynamic state level 2, and Fritchii ships all three unconditionally with its release
notes attributing the black forest fog to the blend case. The effect on this round's two screenshots
is not measured. It is the strongest remaining hypothesis for the character lighting collapsing to
black with only the emissive eyes lit, and for the blown-out haze, and it is being tested as such.

diff --git a/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/spirv_emit_context.cpp b/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/spirv_emit_context.cpp
index 7b984343d6..d13d5d1acb 100644
--- a/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/spirv_emit_context.cpp
+++ b/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/spirv_emit_context.cpp
@@ -36,9 +36,14 @@ bool AmdPr273Fixes() {
 }
 
 bool AmdShaderFixes() {
+    // On by default. The device this port runs on is RADV on RDNA2, where a storage-buffer binding
+    // base is aligned only to 4 bytes so a 64/128-bit access can land on any word (Ocarina's
+    // lighting), and where Maxwell's NaN-defining FMNMX/.SAT has no equivalent in Vulkan's
+    // FMin/FMax/FClamp. Fritchii's fork carries both as ordinary renderer behaviour rather than an
+    // option. EDEN_NX2_AMD_SHADER_FIXES=0 turns them off for a bisect.
     static const bool enabled = [] {
         const char* const value = std::getenv("EDEN_NX2_AMD_SHADER_FIXES");
-        return value != nullptr && value[0] != '0';
+        return value == nullptr || value[0] != '0';
     }();
     return enabled;
 }
